WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.301 --> 00:00:02.801
-Our next guest
is an Emmy-nominated actor

00:00:02.801 --> 00:00:05.004
you know from shows like
"The Path" and "Hannibal."

00:00:05.004 --> 00:00:07.840
He stars in the 21st season
of "Law &amp; Order,"

00:00:07.840 --> 00:00:10.143
which airs Thursday nights
right here on NBC.

00:00:10.143 --> 00:00:11.378
Let's take a look.

00:00:11.378 --> 00:00:13.146
-Find out
what they were talking about.

00:00:13.146 --> 00:00:15.748
-Tried. She made herself
part of the defense team,

00:00:15.748 --> 00:00:19.085
filed an appearance
at the arraignment.

00:00:19.085 --> 00:00:22.088
-Okay.

00:00:22.088 --> 00:00:24.657
So let's work with what we have.

00:00:24.657 --> 00:00:27.360
You've tried cases
without motive before.

00:00:27.360 --> 00:00:29.562
-I have,
but not first-degree murder

00:00:29.562 --> 00:00:33.699
and not when the defendant
was a global icon.

00:00:33.699 --> 00:00:35.868
-Just tell the jury
what you have

00:00:35.868 --> 00:00:37.937
and what you don't have.

00:00:37.937 --> 00:00:40.940
-Please welcome back to the show
our friend Hugh Dancy, everyone!

00:00:40.940 --> 00:00:42.942
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:42.942 --> 00:00:52.952 line:0%
♪♪

00:00:52.952 --> 00:00:54.721 line:0%
♪♪

00:00:54.721 --> 00:00:56.656
Welcome.
-Thank you.

00:00:56.656 --> 00:00:59.725
-I was telling you backstage,
I am --

00:00:59.725 --> 00:01:01.694
I'm so happy to have
this franchise back.

00:01:01.694 --> 00:01:03.863
It's so nice
to see Sam Waterston.

00:01:03.863 --> 00:01:05.798
To be in a room with him
must be just a joy.

00:01:05.798 --> 00:01:08.334
-It's incredible.
It was amazing

00:01:08.334 --> 00:01:10.336
being in that room
that you just saw with him

00:01:10.336 --> 00:01:14.207
when he walked back into it
after 11 years, I'm gonna say?

00:01:14.207 --> 00:01:16.041
-Yeah.

00:01:16.041 --> 00:01:19.012
-Which I can only assume
that his mind was melting.

00:01:19.012 --> 00:01:20.813
But you just saw Sam's face.

00:01:20.813 --> 00:01:23.382
You know, impassive, kind of...
-[ Laughs ]

00:01:23.382 --> 00:01:25.818
-Yeah, incredible.
-And this is --

00:01:25.818 --> 00:01:28.421
You know, the last time
you were, I guess,

00:01:28.421 --> 00:01:30.689
probably on an NBC show,
you were FBI profiler

00:01:30.689 --> 00:01:32.258
Will Graham on "Hannibal."

00:01:32.258 --> 00:01:35.428
And a fantastic show.
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:01:35.428 --> 00:01:37.763
And maybe you would think --

00:01:37.763 --> 00:01:39.231
You're currently playing
an A.D.A.,

00:01:39.231 --> 00:01:40.733
obviously dealing
with New York crime.

00:01:40.733 --> 00:01:43.670
And yet maybe easier than what
Will Graham was dealing with.

00:01:43.670 --> 00:01:46.205
-Nobody is trying to eat me.

00:01:46.205 --> 00:01:48.040
-Yeah.
[ Laughter ]

00:01:48.040 --> 00:01:50.476
-That should be the tagline.
-That must be a nice departure.

00:01:50.476 --> 00:01:52.612
-Yes, it is.
-"If you enjoy Hugh Dancy

00:01:52.612 --> 00:01:54.013
and don't want
to see him get eaten,

00:01:54.013 --> 00:01:55.914
this is the show for you,
the new 'Law &amp; Order.'"

00:01:55.914 --> 00:01:57.951
-But I'm not sure that people
did want to see that.

00:01:57.951 --> 00:01:58.951
I don't know.
[ Laughter ]

00:01:58.951 --> 00:02:00.286
But it feels like
a net positive.

00:02:00.286 --> 00:02:01.921
-Yes, it does feel
like a net positive.

00:02:01.921 --> 00:02:03.722
And you get to film
in New York City.

00:02:03.722 --> 00:02:04.857
-Yes.
-And you have obviously

00:02:04.857 --> 00:02:06.358
done theater in New York City.
-Mm-hmm.

00:02:06.358 --> 00:02:10.262
And again, I think one of the
joys of watching "Law &amp; Order,"

00:02:10.262 --> 00:02:12.431
especially over 21 seasons,
is you saw so many

00:02:12.431 --> 00:02:15.702
incredible stage actors populate
that cast over the years.

00:02:15.702 --> 00:02:18.571
-Literally every stage actor.
-Yeah.

00:02:18.571 --> 00:02:21.240
-I mean, I've --
Again, Sam says this.

00:02:21.240 --> 00:02:24.510
He feels that Dick Wolf
should get a Tony

00:02:24.510 --> 00:02:26.745
for his services to the theater.
You know?

00:02:26.745 --> 00:02:28.147
Everybody I've worked with
has been doing --

00:02:28.147 --> 00:02:29.548
Except for me, so now I feel --

00:02:29.548 --> 00:02:31.850
-So, this is a big deal
for you to finally --

00:02:31.850 --> 00:02:33.753
to finally make it
onto a "Law &amp; Order."

00:02:33.753 --> 00:02:35.889
-Yeah. As somebody
who's been lucky enough

00:02:35.889 --> 00:02:37.957
to work in the theater but also
somebody who lives here...

00:02:37.957 --> 00:02:39.158
-Yeah.

00:02:39.158 --> 00:02:40.893
-...it feels a little
validating, for sure.

00:02:40.893 --> 00:02:44.130
-And your wife, Claire Danes,
has been on "Law &amp; Order."

00:02:44.130 --> 00:02:45.964
-I think I'm right in saying
it was her first job.

00:02:45.964 --> 00:02:48.200
-It was her first job?
-Yeah. She played a murderer.

00:02:48.200 --> 00:02:49.369
-Did she really?
[ Laughter ]

00:02:49.369 --> 00:02:50.703
-Yeah, yeah.
-Good for her.

00:02:50.703 --> 00:02:53.172
That's a really good first job.
-Yes! It is.

00:02:53.172 --> 00:02:54.774
-I feel like the best they would
ever give me on "Law &amp; Order"

00:02:54.774 --> 00:02:56.409
is the jogger who sees a body.

00:02:56.409 --> 00:02:57.610
[ Laughter ]

00:02:57.610 --> 00:02:59.178
-You could be on the jury.
-I wouldn't --

00:02:59.178 --> 00:03:01.447
I'd want to be the jogger.
I want to be the jogger

00:03:01.447 --> 00:03:04.416
who's talking to another jogger
about some real, you know, B.S.,

00:03:04.416 --> 00:03:06.051
and then, all of a sudden,
it's like, "Oh, my God!"

00:03:06.051 --> 00:03:07.420
[ Laughter ]
-Yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:03:07.420 --> 00:03:09.155
-Hard cut,
then I'm talking to someone.

00:03:09.155 --> 00:03:10.557
I'm like,
"Yeah, I was just jogging."

00:03:10.557 --> 00:03:12.091
-In the background,
talking to you.

00:03:12.091 --> 00:03:15.227
-Now I'm in the background,
overacting.

00:03:15.227 --> 00:03:16.629
[ Laughter ]
-Yeah.

00:03:16.629 --> 00:03:18.597
I mean, again, somebody
has to do that job, you know?

00:03:18.597 --> 00:03:19.932
-Somebody has to do that job.

00:03:19.932 --> 00:03:22.001
Well, you know Dick Wolf now.
Put in a word for me.

00:03:22.001 --> 00:03:23.669
-Done.
-Thank you.

00:03:23.669 --> 00:03:26.839
I would imagine it's also
really cool to shoot on --

00:03:26.839 --> 00:03:28.641
You know, the New York City
streets are your set.

00:03:28.641 --> 00:03:30.676
-Yeah.
I mean, the advantage --

00:03:30.676 --> 00:03:33.345
I play, as you say, the A.D.A.,
so I'm prosecuting.

00:03:33.345 --> 00:03:35.181
I'm in, like, the second half
of the show, mostly.

00:03:35.181 --> 00:03:37.684
Lawyers, it turns out,
are not the ones

00:03:37.684 --> 00:03:39.752
that go out into Central Park
at midnight.

00:03:39.752 --> 00:03:41.754
-That's true.
-Which is great for me.

00:03:41.754 --> 00:03:43.389
-Yeah.
-It's --

00:03:43.389 --> 00:03:44.958
That's Anthony Anderson
and Jeffrey Donovan.

00:03:44.958 --> 00:03:46.292
They're out there
doing that good stuff,

00:03:46.292 --> 00:03:48.127
and I'm mostly in the studio.

00:03:48.127 --> 00:03:52.665
But it's great --
Just again, the New York --

00:03:52.665 --> 00:03:55.802
Just before I came here,
I was in an Italian butcher's,

00:03:55.802 --> 00:03:58.137
and a woman turned to me
and said,

00:03:58.137 --> 00:03:59.605
"You're doing great on 'L&amp;O.'"

00:03:59.605 --> 00:04:01.006
[ Laughter ]

00:04:01.006 --> 00:04:05.044
And I thought she said, "You
were great on the 'Ellen' show."

00:04:05.044 --> 00:04:06.345
-Yeah.
[ Laughter ]

00:04:06.345 --> 00:04:09.715
-And I thought to myself --
This is like an hour ago,

00:04:09.715 --> 00:04:11.183
and I thought, "I was great
on the 'Ellen' show,

00:04:11.183 --> 00:04:12.551
but it was in 2003."

00:04:12.551 --> 00:04:13.719
-Yeah.
[ Laughter ]

00:04:13.719 --> 00:04:16.288
-So that's a long memory
she's got.

00:04:16.288 --> 00:04:19.058
-I wish you'd overreacted and
said, "I've waited so long..."

00:04:19.058 --> 00:04:20.226
[ Laughter ]
-Exactly.

00:04:20.226 --> 00:04:21.360
-"...for someone
to give me that feedback."

00:04:21.360 --> 00:04:23.162
-"Thank you, madam!"
[ Laughs ]

00:04:23.162 --> 00:04:26.465
-You're also in the new
"Downton Abbey" film.

00:04:26.465 --> 00:04:28.334
That, I guess, is a little bit
more like your roots,

00:04:28.334 --> 00:04:30.269
a little bit more costume drama,

00:04:30.269 --> 00:04:31.704
which you did
back in the early days.

00:04:31.704 --> 00:04:35.274
Was it nice to get back in those
sort of British period pieces?

00:04:35.274 --> 00:04:38.111
-It was.
It was --

00:04:38.111 --> 00:04:41.981
That kind of dropped out of
the sky for me, post-pandemic.

00:04:41.981 --> 00:04:46.886
So there was a kind of feeling
of massive relief, honestly,

00:04:46.886 --> 00:04:49.021
that I was working,
but a strange feeling

00:04:49.021 --> 00:04:50.523
that I had been
transported back 20 years

00:04:50.523 --> 00:04:53.025
in time in my own career.
-Yeah.

00:04:53.025 --> 00:04:57.429
-But then, you know, to find
myself in that stately home,

00:04:57.429 --> 00:05:02.235
surrounded by all those icons,
you know, sipping tea

00:05:02.235 --> 00:05:04.904
and kind of gossiping in between
takes, was amazing.

00:05:04.904 --> 00:05:06.773
-Your boys are 9 and 3.

00:05:06.773 --> 00:05:09.008
Do they engage
with your work at all?

00:05:09.008 --> 00:05:11.344
Is there anything of yours
that they care about?

00:05:11.344 --> 00:05:13.312
-Well...
[ Laughter ]

00:05:13.312 --> 00:05:14.614
I mean,
that's a tough question, Seth.

00:05:14.614 --> 00:05:17.216
-It's a tough age.
-Yes, yeah, it is.

00:05:17.216 --> 00:05:20.319
And the youngest one probably
doesn't really know enough

00:05:20.319 --> 00:05:22.155
to care, and the oldest one --

00:05:22.155 --> 00:05:24.757
Well, actually, the oldest one,
I took him -- I went to --

00:05:24.757 --> 00:05:26.959
They screened today.
The people who made it

00:05:26.959 --> 00:05:28.894
screened "Downton Abbey"
for me this morning.

00:05:28.894 --> 00:05:31.797
So before the Italian butchers,
that's where I was.

00:05:31.797 --> 00:05:35.534
And I took him with me --
boldly, some would say --

00:05:35.534 --> 00:05:37.103
because I didn't know
what his response would be.

00:05:37.103 --> 00:05:39.405
But he came to love it.

00:05:39.405 --> 00:05:40.773
-Oh, that's really sweet.
-Yeah.

00:05:40.773 --> 00:05:42.408
-That's all you can ask for.
-He turned to me

00:05:42.408 --> 00:05:45.778
in the middle of the screening
and he said, "This is good."

00:05:45.778 --> 00:05:47.880
[ Laughter ]

00:05:47.880 --> 00:05:50.449
"You're good."
[ Laughter ]

00:05:50.449 --> 00:05:52.985
"You're an actor."
[ Laughter ]

00:05:52.985 --> 00:05:54.687
The last bit
I'm still mulling over.

00:05:54.687 --> 00:05:56.089
-Yeah.
-'Cause it's accurate.

00:05:56.089 --> 00:05:58.224
-Yeah.
-But it's also quite neutral.

00:05:58.224 --> 00:06:00.593
-The thing that just
gave me goose bumps is,

00:06:00.593 --> 00:06:03.529
I feel like
that would be the dream

00:06:03.529 --> 00:06:05.331
I would want my father
to turn to me and say.

00:06:05.331 --> 00:06:06.598
And I'm like, "Wait.
Does that mean you get

00:06:06.598 --> 00:06:09.134
a new father
that's just a young one?"

00:06:09.134 --> 00:06:11.003
-It was so validating.
-Yeah.

00:06:11.003 --> 00:06:13.773
-And then, at the end, I said --
and this is gonna seem desperate

00:06:13.773 --> 00:06:16.408
because it probably was,
but I said,

00:06:16.408 --> 00:06:18.010
"So, what was
your favorite bit?"

00:06:18.010 --> 00:06:19.278
And he said --

00:06:19.278 --> 00:06:22.281
Honestly, he said,
"The bit when they hired you."

00:06:22.281 --> 00:06:23.449
[ Laughter ]

00:06:23.449 --> 00:06:25.251
Which, again,
could have been a plot point

00:06:25.251 --> 00:06:26.719
in the movie when you see it.

00:06:26.719 --> 00:06:30.289
But it was ambiguous.
[ Laughter ]

00:06:30.289 --> 00:06:32.725
"Me, too!"
-"We just need you to work, Dad.

00:06:32.725 --> 00:06:34.460
We just need you
to keep working."

00:06:34.460 --> 00:06:36.195
Hey, congratulations again
on the show.

00:06:36.195 --> 00:06:38.364
It's so nice to see you.
That's Hugh Dancy, everybody.

00:06:38.364 --> 00:06:41.000
"Law &amp; Order"
airs Thursdays at 8:00 p.m.,

00:06:41.000 --> 00:06:43.469
here on NBC.
And "Downton Abbey: A New Era"

00:06:43.469 --> 00:06:45.405
will be in theaters on May 20th.

